{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And this for Judah.</b> That is: 'and this is the blessing' — just as he blessed Reuben, so he blessed Judah, for over both of them he prayed that they succeed in war: over Reuben, as I explained; and over Judah, because he always goes up first in war, as it is written: ״מי יעלה לנו״ — 'Who shall go up for us?' — and He said: ״יהודה יעלה״, etc. Many explain 'upon his bones' — that they were rolling about in the ark, because he said: ״וחטאתי לאבי כל הימים״. For even though it appears that they returned him to Benjamin, since he was counted among those who went down to Egypt and not with Joseph and his sons who were in Egypt — nevertheless, the curse of a sage comes even conditionally. And so we find in the second chapter of Makkot: when David dug sixty cubits into the deep and sought to flood the world, David opened and said: 'Whoever knows a thing, let him come and revoke it' — but he did not say 'let him be strangled in his throat.' And Achitophel was there, and he began to write 'there' [שם] and cast it upon the deep, as it is written: ״ויצו אל ביתו ויחנק״. And in the second chapter of Makkot it is further concluded that Moses said concerning the bones of Judah rolling in the ark: 'What caused Reuben to confess publicly regarding the disordering of his father's bed? Judah, who confessed publicly regarding the matter of Tamar.' And וזאת ליהודה — in astonishment: ״שמע ה' קול יהודה״, etc., as is brought there. And Simeon he did not bless, just as his father did not bless him, because of the matter of Shechem, for he said: ״אתם עכרתם אותי״. And moreover he added to his sin by transgressing in the matter of Zimri. And even though Levi was also in that matter of Shechem and his father did not bless him, nevertheless Moses blessed him, because he rectified the wrong in the [golden] calf, as it is said: ״ויאספו אליו כל בני לוי״. Howbeit, his blessing is hinted at somewhat, as it is said: ״שמע ה' קול יהודה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The closing citation שמע ה׳ קול יהודה appears to be attributed by the compiler to Levi's blessing as a hint, though the verse itself belongs to Judah's blessing in the immediate context; the logic appears to be that since Moses grouped Judah and Simeon together (both unblessed by Jacob), Levi's inclusion is also thematically linked — the passage is dense and possibly conflates multiple opinions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:08.405543744Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}